Three composable edits in src/LacertMCP.Cli/Commands/FieldAccessCommand.cs, all sim-only:

(1) Add a private static helper near the bottom of the class:
```csharp
private static bool ValidateTable(int table, TextWriter stderr) {
    if (table < 1 || table > 7) {
        stderr.WriteLine($"--table must be in 1..7 (got {table}). DATA tables are numbered 1=Client, 2=SubClient, 3=State, 4=StateSubClient, 5=Status, 6=Display, 7=Forms.");
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```
Call it at the top of RunList (after the `FileBasenameFor` check, before computing the prefix) and at the top of RunSet (after the basename check, before the access enum parse). Return 1 on failure.

(2) Field-name shape check in RunSet only (RunList doesn't take --field). Accept the convention `^[A-Za-z][1-7]_\d+$` (matches `C1_42`, `c1_42`, `I3_0`). On mismatch:
```csharp
stderr.WriteLine($"--field '{field}' does not match the <letter><table>_<n> convention (e.g. C1_42). Pass --force to override.");
return 1;
```
Add a `--force` switch (Option<bool>, default false) wired through to a new `bool force` parameter on the public RunSet overload so the escape hatch is explicit and grep-able. Do NOT regex-gate RunList â€” it's read-only and the existing key prefix filter already silently no-ops a typo.

(3) Reword the universal-rule message on line 105 to be module-agnostic and stop using the misleading `C` prefix on non-C modules:
```csharp
writer.WriteLine($"Universal rule: field _0 on every DATA table is ReadOnly (always, no entry needed).");
```
or, if you want to keep the example, `Universal rule: any field ending in _0 (e.g. {module}{table}_0) is ReadOnly on every module â€” no entry needed.` The `{module}{table}_0` form makes the example correct for the actual module the user queried.

(4) Add three tiny regression tests in src/LacertMCP.Tests/Cli/FieldAccessCommandTests.cs (the file the P15-067 finding seeded the pattern for):
- `RunList_TableOutOfRange_PrintsErrorAndExitsOne` â€” invoke with table=99, assert exit 1 + stderr contains "1..7", stdout empty.
- `RunSet_TableOutOfRange_DoesNotMutateShard` â€” capture shard mtime before/after, assert unchanged and exit 1.
- `RunSet_FieldShapeMismatch_RejectsWithoutForce_AcceptsWithForce` â€” first call exits 1 with `oops_typo`; second call with --force writes the row.

Total diff ~50 lines incl. tests. No SDK, no Windows, no observable shape change for any happy-path caller.

